  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday Night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1137 PM CDT Wed Apr 22 2026\n\nBy far, the better potential for thunderstorm development and\nsevere weather on Thursday will hold off until the evening and\novernight hours along the cold front. Development should begin\nduring the early evening hours across parts of Kansas in response\nto a disturbance moving through the region. The expected line of\nthunderstorms will move to the southeast, with parts of northeast\nOklahoma and northwest Arkansas most likely to see these\nthunderstorms prior to daybreak Friday. Locally damaging winds\nshould be the primary severe weather threat. Conditions do not\nlook completely ideal in this set up for leading edge tornadoes\nbut with a significant amount of low level shear, a low threat for\nsuch will exist. Storms will continue into Friday morning across\neastern parts of the area, although waning in intensity with time. \nThe cold front will stall across the area, likely near to just \nsouth of I-40, for Friday afternoon, providing the focus for \nadditional thunderstorm development during the afternoon and \nevening hours. The afternoon and evening storms will have another \nthreat for severe weather. This should also push the front to the\nvicinity of the Red River late Friday night into early Saturday\nmorning. As another wave approaches from the southwest Saturday\ninto Sunday, the front will move northward once again.\nThunderstorm chances will increase once again Saturday afternoon,\nbut primarily Saturday night into Sunday morning. Sunday afternoon\nand evening, yet another round of thunderstorms looks to occur \nahead of the dryline, with instability and shear parameters\nfavoring tornadoes, in addition to large hail and damaging winds. \n\nThe active weather period should briefly end Monday into Tuesday\nas a cold front moves through the region bringing cooler and \ndrier air. The break will be short-lived, however, as southerly\nwinds develop again, bringing moisture and shower and thunderstorm\nchances back to the region. Although details are less than clear\nthis far out, a threat for severe weather may develop once again \nduring this time period.",
